{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg1M2thDqS3qGFEwnEgU8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I give up on this one. I did not modify `/work/combi_imo_2008_p5.lean`, so it still contains the original `sorry`.\n\nThe statement is true, and I have a mathematical route, but I don't think I can get a complete Lean proof in this session.\n\n**Why it's true.** Lamp `i`'s final state is the parity of how many times it was switched. So `N` counts length-`k` words over the `2n` lamps where lamps `1..n` have odd counts and lamps `n+1..2n` have even counts. `M` counts the words over lamps `1..n` only, where every count is odd. Let `F(k; a, b)` be the number of words with `a` labelled odd-count letters and `b` labelled even-count letters. Its exponential generating function is `k! [x^k] sinh^a(x) cosh^b(x)`. Since `sinh(x) cosh(x) = sinh(2x)/2`, this gives `2^n · N = 2^k · M` for all `k`. The parity condition `k - n` even only matters because otherwise both sides are 0. So `N/M = 2^(k-n)`, which matches `imo_2008_p5_solution`.\n\n**Why I can't finish it here.** A Lean proof would need several substantial pieces:\n1. Show `lamps_final_state` equals the parity vector of letter counts. This requires reasoning about `List.mapIdx` and `List.ofFn`.\n2. Turn the `Finset.filter` cardinalities over `Fin k → Fin (2n)` into a counting recurrence, decomposing by the first letter (`Fin.cons`).\n3. Prove the identity `2^n · N = 2^k · M` by induction. A plain recurrence on `F(k; a, b)` does not close on the `(n, n)` and `(n, 0)` cases alone, so the induction needs a more general statement. Reaching that statement is where I see the real difficulty, and I have not found a clean inductive form for it.\n4. Convert the real-valued quotient `N / M` using `M > 0` and the identity from step 3.\n\nSteps 1, 2 and 4 are fiddly but routine. Step 3 is where I don't yet have a clean inductive argument, and that is the main reason I'm st